body:
Points of discussion:

- It may be better to use `IList<>` rather than `List<>` to support other list types such as `Godot.Collections.Array<>`. Or `IList` could be used to support `Godot.Collections.Array`. In fact it looks like `ICollection<>` could be used which would support `HashSet<>`.

- Seeing as `CastMotionResult` is an alternative to an array, would it make sense to add array-like methods?
```cs
public record struct CastMotionResult(
	float Safe,
	float Unsafe
)
{
	public float this[int i]
	{
		readonly get => i switch
		{
			0 => Safe,
			1 => Unsafe,
			_ => throw new IndexOutOfRangeException()
		};
		set => _ = i switch
		{
			0 => Safe = value,
			1 => Unsafe = value,
			_ => throw new IndexOutOfRangeException()
		};
	}
	public int Count => 2;
}
```

- Seeing as these non-allocating APIs have different signatures, would it make sense to implement them on the C++ side to be exposed to GDScript?

- These methods wrap around C++ methods returning `Godot.Collections.Dictionary`. Should those methods even return dictionaries in the first place? It could be a better idea to return an object at the root rather than digging the hole deeper.

- Is `{Method}NonAlloc` the best name for these APIs? It's used by Unity but it feels clunky. Maybe it should be an overload of the original method? Either way, I'm not a big fan of having two methods that do the same thing just one is more performant but has more boilerplate.

- `maxResults` is ambiguous in `IntersectPointNonAlloc` if the list already contains elements.

- An alternative approach could be to have a specialised `struct`-based list type that's doesn't allocate when the number of elements is low. See [`ValueCollections`](https://github.com/Joy-less/ValueCollections) for inspiration.

> That's correct, but I don't like the cost of making a virtual call over the interface, as the performance is critical here.

You can consider using generics, which would make the method signature more complicated but would be just as simple to call due to generic type inference. Then it would support non-`List` types.

```cs
// Old
public unsafe void CollideShapeNonAlloc(PhysicsShapeQueryParameters3D parameters, List<Vector3> results, int maxResults = 32) {
    // ...
}

// New
public unsafe void CollideShapeNonAlloc<TCollection>(PhysicsShapeQueryParameters3D parameters, TCollection results, int maxResults = 32) where TCollection : ICollection<Vector3> {
    // ...
}
```

> You can consider using generics, which would make the method signature more complicated but would be just as simple to call due to generic type inference. Then it would support non-List types.

That's not true, generics with interface constraints are still lowered into the same ICollection::Add by the compiler, consider this example:

```csharp
static void GenericMethod<TCollection>(TCollection collection) where TCollection : ICollection<float>
{
    for (var i = 0; i < 1000; i++)
    {
        collection.Add(i * 0.5f);
    }
}

static void InterfaceMethod(ICollection<float> collection)
{
    for (var i = 0; i < 1000; i++)
    {
        collection.Add(i * 0.5f);
    }
}

static void DirectCallMethod(List<float> collection)
{
    for (var i = 0; i < 1000; i++)
    {
        collection.Add(i * 0.5f);
    }
}
```

If we look at the generated il for the `Add` call, they look like this:

```il
// GenericMethod<TCollection>
      IL_0004: ldarga.s     collection
      IL_0006: ldloc.0      // i
      IL_0007: conv.r4
      IL_0008: ldc.r4       0.5
      IL_000d: mul
      IL_000e: constrained. !!0/*TCollection*/
      IL_0014: callvirt     instance void class [System.Runtime]System.Collections.Generic.ICollection`1<float32>::Add(!0/*float32*/)

// InterfaceMethod
      IL_0004: ldarg.0      // collection
      IL_0005: ldloc.0      // i
      IL_0006: conv.r4
      IL_0007: ldc.r4       0.5
      IL_000c: mul
      IL_000d: callvirt     instance void class [System.Runtime]System.Collections.Generic.ICollection`1<float32>::Add(!0/*float32*/)

// DirectCallMethod
      IL_0004: ldarg.0      // collection
      IL_0005: ldloc.0      // i
      IL_0006: conv.r4
      IL_0007: ldc.r4       0.5
      IL_000c: mul
      IL_000d: callvirt     instance void class [System.Collections]System.Collections.Generic.List`1<float32>::Add(!0/*float32*/)
```

> That way, there would be no risk of growing a list (and reducing performance) during an Add operation. It would also work for arrays, lists, and stackalloc spans.

Yes... but that's yet another `snprintf` -like situation, where I need to call this method twice if I want to ensure I'm getting every results for sure, think about the following:

```csharp
int GetChildrenNonAlloc(Span<Node> results, <omited for examples>)
```

Let's say I have no idea on how many children I currently have, so I have to either:

```csharp
var childrenCount = node.GetChildCount();
var arrayPoolArray = ArrayPool<Node>.Shared.Rent(childrenCount);
node.GetChildrenNonAlloc(arrayPoolArray);
// consume
ArrayPool<Node>.Shared.Return(arrayPoolArray);
```

Or for the cases where there is no such builtin apis for knowing the exact count of the results, I need to call the actual non-alloc api twice:

```csharp
var childrenCount = map.GetChildrenNonAlloc(null);
var arrayPoolArray = ArrayPool<Node>.Shared.Rent(childrenCount);
map.GetChildrenNonAlloc(arrayPoolArray);
// consume
ArrayPool<Node>.Shared.Return(arrayPoolArray);
```

The first one involves a dedicated engine side query + inteop call, the the second one involves performing everything twice, which I don't think is worth it when I want the target collection to adapt based on the actual results.

That non-alloc interop is now fully removed. I also don't think storing logic via a delegate is a performant option as delegate inlining isn't possible in .NET 8 (it comets with .NET 10), so I dropped that too; the non-alloc pattern is now two very simple wrappers within that file:
```csharp
file static class NativeDictionary
{
    [MethodImpl(MethodImplOptions.AggressiveInlining)]
    public static bool IsEmpty(scoped ref godot_dictionary dictionary) =>
        NativeFuncs.godotsharp_dictionary_count(ref dictionary) == 0;

    [MethodImpl(MethodImplOptions.AggressiveInlining)]
    public static bool TryGetValue(scoped ref godot_dictionary dictionary, Variant key, out godot_variant value) =>
        NativeFuncs.godotsharp_dictionary_try_get_value(ref dictionary, key.NativeVar.DangerousSelfRef, out value).ToBool();
}

file static class NativeArray
{
    [MethodImpl(MethodImplOptions.AggressiveInlining)]
    public static unsafe void Fill<[MustBeVariant] T>(scoped ref godot_array array, List<T> results)
    {
        ArgumentNullException.ThrowIfNull(results);

        results.EnsureCapacity(results.Count + array.Size);

        for (var i = 0; i < array.Size; i++)
        {
            results.Add(VariantUtils.ConvertTo<T>(array.Elements[i]));
        }
    }
}
```

And call patterns are now:
```csharp
partial class Node
{
    /// <summary>
    /// <para>Stores all children of this node into the provided <paramref name="results"/> list.</para>
    /// <para>If <paramref name="includeInternal"/> is <see langword="false"/>, excludes internal children when populating the list (see <see cref="Godot.Node.AddChild(Node, bool, Node.InternalMode)"/>'s <c>internal</c> parameter).</para>
    /// </summary>
    public unsafe void GetChildrenNonAlloc(List<Node> results, bool includeInternal = false)
    {
        var instancePtr = GetPtr(this);
        ExceptionUtils.ThrowIfNullPtr(instancePtr);
        var callArgs = stackalloc void*[1] { &includeInternal };
        godot_array ret = default;
        NativeFuncs.godotsharp_method_bind_ptrcall(MethodBind9, instancePtr, callArgs, &ret);
        try
        {
            NativeArray.Fill(ref ret, results);
        }
        finally
        {
            ret.Dispose();
        }
    }
}
```

Which may also improve perf.

Benchmark context

