Benchmark code:

```csharp
	[GlobalSetup]
	public void GlobalSetup()
	{
		_space = Space ?? throw new InvalidOperationException("RaycastApiOverheadBenchmarks.Space was not set by BenchmarkHost.");
		_query = Query ?? throw new InvalidOperationException("RaycastApiOverheadBenchmarks.Query was not set by BenchmarkHost.");
		_rayCast = RayCast ?? throw new InvalidOperationException("RaycastApiOverheadBenchmarks.RayCast was not set by BenchmarkHost.");
	}

	[Benchmark(Baseline = true)]
	public Dictionary IntersectRay_Alloc() => _space.IntersectRay(_query);

	[Benchmark]
	public PhysicsDirectSpaceState3D.IntersectRayResult? IntersectRay_NonAlloc() =>
		_space.IntersectRayNonAlloc(_query);

	[Benchmark]
	public bool RayCast3D_ForceUpdate()
	{
		_rayCast.ForceRaycastUpdate();
		return _rayCast.IsColliding();
	}
```

@Komariarii 

After a complete warm-up, the Node approach demonstrates the best performance. This makes sense, as most raycast results occur on the C++ side, with only a boolean value being passed through the interop layer.

The performance of Alloc vs. Non-Alloc is largely similar, except for the impact on GC pressure. The underlying mechanisms for both approaches are identical, as they both involve transferring a full `IntersectRayResult` across the interop layer.
